having your picture taken is part of the  job of being the president  sometimes photographers capture the  tensest moments of the office or some of  the lighter side of the presidency but  now we're getting a look at what's  believed to be the oldest surviving  photograph of an American president this  early daguerreotype of the sixth  President John Quincy Adams was taken in  March of 1843 about fourteen years after  he left the White House according to an  entry in Adams journal quote the  operation is performed in half a minute  but is yet altogether incomprehensible  to me fascinated by the process  Adams gifted the photo to his friend  Horace Everett a congressman from  Vermont the five by four inch picture  was found by one of Everett's  descendants in the 90s and now it's  headed for auction at Sotheby's the  importance of this photograph is that it  is a rare early American portrait it's a  historical object as well Adams who was  notoriously picky about how he looked in  portraits and photos seemed to like this  one enough to give it as a gift to have  the ability to see who he was in a  photograph is really special he is not  just an idea here he is the man himself  the auction is in October in the  estimate is may go for up to $250,000 it  looks a little grumpy in that photograph  I thought yeah I'm actually just amazed  that it's survived over time you think  of those photos have disappeared that  itself is extraordinary yeah
 

